I see the key issue now! Look at `ofpbuf_resize__`:

```c
case OFPBUF_STUB:
    b->source = OFPBUF_MALLOC;
    new_base = xmalloc(new_allocated);
    ofpbuf_copy__(b, new_base, new_headroom, new_tailroom);
    break;
```

When the stub buffer overflows, it changes `b->source` to `OFPBUF_MALLOC`, allocates a new heap buffer, and copies the data there. But the original `metadata` stack pointer is NOT updated. After `ofpbuf_uninit(&b)`, the heap buffer is freed, and `metadata` still points to the original 248-byte stack buffer.

But `md_size` is set to `mdlen + padding` which reflects the actual decoded data length. So when `nsh_key_to_attr` is called with `metadata` and `md_size`, it reads `md_size` bytes from the 248-byte stack buffer.

This should definitely cause a stack-buffer-overflow READ when ASan is enabled. But the exit code was 0...

Let me check if maybe the issue is that the `ofpbuf_put_hex` function doesn't actually decode all 255 bytes because of some limitation. Let me check `hexits_value`:

